+\title{Sequential Training of Windowed Physics-Informed Neural Networks for the Lorenz-1960 Ordinary Differential Equation System}
+
+\author{}
 
 \maketitle
 
 \begin{abstract}
-We study a physics-informed neural network (PINN) approximation of a single nearly closed orbit of the Lorenz three-state system on $t\in[0,13.26446]$. We trained a single network on the full interval that produces a combined state root-mean-square error (RMSE) of 2.237. To address this long horizon problem, we divide the interval into 27 windows. Each window has a separate network and an initial state that is fixed to the predicted endpoint of the previous window. In each window, Adam reduces the residual in four stages. In each stage, we use causal weights and causal weighted loss to reduce the loss of all earlier points. Then, L-BFGS minimizes the loss using the unweighted residual mean. A numerical reference is used to monitor the training but is not used for optimization or stopping. Across five seeds, the windowed model gives a median combined RMSE of $8.13\times10^{-5}$ on 13,265 evaluation times. Also a dense-grid residual mean square of $1.87\times10^{-7}$. The full-interval and windowed runs also vary in number of parameters, sampling and optimizer schedule, so we describe them as different configurations. Ablations show that separate window networks is needed and warm start improves accuracy and reduced training steps. 
+We study a physics-informed neural network (PINN) approximation of a single nearly closed orbit of the Lorenz three-state system on the time interval from 0 to 13.26446. We trained a single network on the full interval that produces a combined state root-mean-square error (RMSE) of 2.237. To address this long horizon problem, we divide the interval into 27 windows. Each window has a separate network and an initial state that is fixed to the predicted endpoint of the previous window. In each window, Adam reduces the residual in four stages. In each stage, we use causal weights and causal weighted loss to reduce the loss of all earlier points. Then, limited-memory Broyden–Fletcher–Goldfarb–Shanno (L-BFGS) minimizes the loss using the unweighted residual mean. A numerical reference is used to monitor the training but is not used for optimization or stopping. Across five seeds, the windowed model gives a median combined RMSE of 0.0000813 on 13,265 evaluation times. Also a dense-grid residual mean square of 0.000000187. The full-interval and windowed runs also vary in number of parameters, sampling and optimizer schedule, so we describe them as different configurations. Ablations show that separate window networks is needed and warm start improves accuracy and reduced training steps.
 
 \end{abstract}
 
